th of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-LYVHBWDJ' (errno=Operation not permitted)\n2026-11-02 14:30:04.830 xcodebuild[23340:105350176]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\n2026-11-02 14:30:05.075 xcodebuild[23340:105350174] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \"Input/output error\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\n?? collab/1793402000000-architect-adopt-rebased-confirm.gpt-6-astra.findings.md\n?? collab/1793466815000-reviewer-nightly-testing-plan.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md\n?? collab/1793466815000-reviewer-nightly-testing-plan.gpt-6.1-sol.prompt.md\n?? collab/1793466815000-reviewer-nightly-testing-plan.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log\n?? collab/1793470291000-engineer-ts-t3-s10-v2.gpt-6-luna.findings.md\n?? collab/1793470291000-engineer-ts-t3-s10-v2.gpt-6-luna.stdout.log\n?? collab/1793470291000-engineer-ts-t3-s10-v2.prompt.md\n?? collab/1793512000000-reviewer-nightly-plan-re-review.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log\n?? collab/1793512000000-reviewer-nightly-plan-re-review.prompt.md\n?? collab/1793514000000-reviewer-node-shard-diagnosis.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md\n?? collab/1793514000000-reviewer-node-shard-diagnosis.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log\n?? collab/1793514000000-reviewer-node-shard-diagnosis.kimi-k3.1-pro.stdout.log\n?? collab/1793514000000-reviewer-node-shard-diagnosis.prompt.md\n?? collab/1793516000000-engineer-node-shard-daostream-fix.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md\n?? collab/1793516000000-engineer-node-shard-daostream-fix.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log\n?? collab/1793516000000-engineer-node-shard-daostream-fix.prompt.md\n?? collab/1793518000000-engineer-dart-shard-diagnosis.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md\n?? collab/1793518000000-engineer-dart-shard-diagnosis.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log\n?? collab/1793518000000-engineer-dart-shard-diagnosis.prompt.md\n?? collab/1793522000000-reviewer-testing-process-policy.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md\n?? collab/1793522000000-reviewer-testing-process-policy.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log\n?? collab/1793522000000-reviewer-testing-process-policy.prompt.md\n?? collab/1793522867000-orchestrator-call-python-clojure-handoff.prompt.md\n?? collab/1793560086786-investigator-datagram-linux-diagnosis.gpt-6-astra.findings.md\n?? collab/1793560086786-investigator-datagram-linux-diagnosis.prompt.md\n?? collab/1793610000000-orchestrator-to-ts-php-crosslang-conformance-gap.md\n?? collab/1793620000000-engineer-ts-yangcall-routing.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md\n?? collab/1793676800000-architect-yang-h2-reconcile.gpt-6-astra.prompt.md\n?? collab/1793676800000-architect-yang-h2-reconcile.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log\n?? collab/1793688000000-architect-yang-h2-reconcile-r2.gpt-6-astra.prompt.md\n?? collab/1793688000000-architect-yang-h2-reconcile-r2.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log\n?? collab/1793700000000-architect-yang-h2-reconcile-r3.gpt-6-astra.prompt.md\n?? collab/1793700000000-architect-yang-h2-reconcile-r3.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log\n?? collab/1793710000000-architect-yang-h2-reconcile-r4.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log\n?? collab/1793740000000-engineer-gen-antlr-prereq-heuristic.codex.stdout.log\n?? collab/1793740000000-engineer-gen-antlr-prereq-heuristic.prompt.md\n?? collab/1793770000000-architect-hardening-3-design.gpt-6-astra.prompt.md\n?? collab/1793770000000-architect-hardening-3-design.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log\n?? collab/yang-clojure/1793740000000-engineer-gen-antlr-prereq-heuristic.codex.stdout.log\n?? collab/yang-clojure/1793740000000-engineer-gen-antlr-prereq-heuristic.prompt.md\n?? target-ci/node-shards-20261101-0100/npm-ci.log\n?? target-ci/node-shards-20261101-0100/summary.txt\n?? target-ci/node-shards-20261101-0104/npm-ci.log\n?? target-ci/node-shards-20261101-0104/summary.txt\n?? target-ci/node-shards-latest\n---\ndescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\n---\n\n# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\n\n## Core Philosophy\n\nEverything is data.\nInterpretation > abstraction.\nExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\nRestrictions are a feature.\n\n### Foundational Axioms\n\n1. **Everything is a Stream**: Portable I/O and system data flow through append-only streams; hosts may also install explicit local functions that perform direct I/O.\n2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\n   At system scale, a \"system\" is an emergent property of interpreters of\n   pure data, not an object, service, or stored artifact. Find the\n   interpreters and the data they share to find the system.\n3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\n4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\n\n## Non-Negotiable Invariants\n\n- Do not introduce hidden global state.\n- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\n- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\n- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\n- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\n- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\n- Do not lose information (unitarity): streams are append-only and no datom is destroyed, only reinterpreted. The Universal AST is never lost: it is always retrievable wherever code is lowered, by address or by a query over its datoms. Every derived form (semantic vector, native images, indexes) is an interpretation of it and may drop facts only because the AST remains.\n\n## Tuples and Datoms\n\nTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\nA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\nSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\n\nSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\n\n## Streams\n\n`dao.stream` is an I/O monad and the system's portable I/O ABI. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\nHosts may install arbitrary local functions, including direct I/O; their availability and migration semantics must be explicit.\nPortable behavior cannot assume them; portable side effects appear as stream emissions. Direct-I/O host modules are explicitly local and non-migratable (owner decision, 2026-10-23).\nStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\nStreams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\n\nA stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\nof interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\nsemantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\nstream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\nthe very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\nanswers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\nexists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\nthe other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\nand the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\nbecomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\nthe stream's own.\n\nFunctions can take anything and return anything.\nAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate portably with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Portable agent behavior is specified through stream effects; host-local functions may also perform direct I/O.\n\nSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\nSee [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\n\n### Host Boundaries\n\nA host boundary is the dao.stream abstraction boundary; implementations do not add contracts.\n[datom.world.abi.md](./datom.world.abi.md) owns the doctrine, owner rulings, taxonomy, floor and custody rules.\n\n## Architectural Commitments\n\n- **`dao.jing` is syntax, agents are semantics**: the content-addressed encoding stays passive and payload-agnostic.\n- **Macros are stream topology**: an evaluator knows nothing about macro expansion; expansion happens on the syntax side of a medium boundary, upstream of any evaluator.\n- **Peer observers, one stream**: a generic evaluator and `dao.space` independently observe the same stream topic, as described under Streams. This is not a claim about any two consumers regardless of what they consume.\n- **Observers are independent and optional**: nothing requires a medium to have any particular set of consumers. Which observers exist for a stream is a per-composition choice, not a design-time commitment.\n\n## Design Principles\n\nCode quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?\nStigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.\n\n**Systems emerge from interpreters of pure data.** Streams are the\ncomposition medium; each interpreter gives the same data a perspective\nwithout collapsing interpretation into execution or owning the truth.\n\n- `dao.space` emerges from `dao.space.index` and `dao.space.query` over\n  ordinary streams.\n- ShiBi capability, metering, and budget emerge from different\n  interpreters over the same tuples; no token object is stored.\n- The four VMs interpret one Universal AST; many syntaxes feed that AST.\n- The semantic AST in `dao.space` is queryable facts projected by the\n  auto-index observer.\n- Client and server are roles in one stigmergic behavior implemented by\n  interpreters, not distinct kinds of nodes.\n\n**Derive, don't persist.** Before adding a tag, slot, or field to any\ncanonical content-hashed structure (an AST grammar, a datom schema, a row\nshape), check whether a correctly scoped Datalog query over\n`dao.space.query/q` can already compute the fact. If it can, it belongs as a\nquery or projection: new persisted structure migrates every downstream\nconsumer and duplicates authority over a fact a query derives fresh. The\nretired `:global` tag in\n[`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md) is the\ncase study, including the scoping bug that a naive query introduced.\n\nSee [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).\nSee [`docs/agents/vocabulary.md`](../agents/vocabulary.md) for domain vocabulary reference (biology, economics, physics, philosophy).\n\n## Code Style\n\n- Prefer simple data over rich types.\n- Prefer total functions over partial ones.\n- Prefer declarative pipelines over imperative logic.\n- Avoid cleverness.\n- Name things after what they do to streams.\n\n## Development Workflow\n\n- Do not invent abstractions that hide streams.\n- Do not suggest mainstream frameworks unless explicitly asked.\n- Do not optimize prematurely.\n- When uncertain, ask at the architectural level, not the implementation level.\n- Explanations should align with Plan 9 / Datomic / Lisp lineage.\n- Program store writes go through `engine/store-put`; any other store write\n  the store-write audit (`test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj`)\n  detects must be on its allowlist; its docstring states the residual it\n  leaves to review.\n\n### Problem Decomposition\n\nIf a problem appears complex:\n1. Decompose it into streams.\n2. Identify invariants.\n3. Restrict degrees of freedom.\n4. Make causality explicit.\n5. Let structure emerge from constraints.\n\n## Operational & Specialized Guides\n\n- **Advanced Concepts**: See [`docs/agents/advanced-concepts.md`](../agents/advanced-concepts.md) for parallel transport, capability tokens, and entanglement.\n- **Build & Test**: See [`docs/agents/build-n-test.md`](../agents/build-n-test.md) for build, lint, and test commands and TDD guidelines.\n- **Engineering Team Roster**: See [`docs/agents/team.md`](../agents/team.md) for engineering team roles and LLM model assignments.\n- **Agent Delegation**: See [`docs/agents/roles/orchestrator.md`](../agents/roles/orchestrator.md) for collaboration protocols and delegating tasks or reviews through `agy`, `cmd`, `glm`, `deepseek`, and `codex`; each orchestrator session has its own append-only log at `orchestrator-logs/<unix-time-stamp>-<brief-orchestrator-descriptive-name>.md` under the repository root.\n- **Website & Content**: See [`docs/agents/format.md`](../agents/format.md) for `.chp` and `.blog` EDN/Hiccup file specifications.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"wc -l docs/design/yin.cic.spec.md test/yang/latte_oracle/slice9_gate.md test/yang/latte_oracle/slice9_evidence.edn test/yang/latte_oracle/slice9_unexercised.edn docs/agents/known-gaps.md; cat test/yang/latte_oracle/slice9_gate.md; cat test/yang/latte_oracle/slice9_unexercised.edn; rg -n 'yang.clojure|8\\\\.10|instruction-budget|recorded-portability|mismatch|blocked|failure|exception' docs/design/yin.cic.spec.md docs/agents/known-gaps.md test/yang/latte_oracle/slice9_evidence.edn\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"wc -l docs/design/yin.cic.spec.md test/yang/latte_oracle/slice9_gate.md test/yang/latte_oracle/slice9_evidence.edn test/yang/latte_oracle/slice9_unexercised.edn docs/agents/known-gaps.md; cat test/yang/latte_oracle/slice9_gate.md; cat test/yang/latte_oracle/slice9_unexercised.edn; rg -n 'yang.clojure|8\\\\.10|instruction-budget|recorded-portability|mismatch|blocked|failure|exception' docs/design/yin.cic.spec.md docs/agents/known-gaps.md test/yang/latte_oracle/slice9_evidence.edn\"","aggregated_output":"     397 docs/design/yin.cic.spec.md\n     164 test/yang/latte_oracle/slice9_gate.md\n     141 test/yang/latte_oracle/slice9_evidence.edn\n       2 test/yang/latte_oracle/slice9_unexercised.edn\n     584 docs/agents/known-gaps.md\n    1288 total\n# Slice 9 kernel differential gate: execution evidence correction\n\nThe gate on `yang-hardening2` is **blocked**. The architect denied the prior\n165/165 closure claim because membership in a static dependency closure did\nnot prove execution of every arity. That claim is withdrawn. The gate retains\nits final assertion `(= :pass (:gate-status evidence))`; missing execution\nevidence must fail that assertion.\n\nThe 106-fixture corpus still has 102 passing agreements, two recorded\ndeviations, and two recorded upstream failures. The four narrowly recorded\nexception dispositions remain declared departures, not agreements. No\nupstream or `src/cljc/` source changes are made. Admission is unchanged:\n155 bindings (146 admitted, 9 excluded) and 175 candidate function/arities\n(165 admitted, 10 excluded).\n\nRecorded executions now establish **145/165** admitted arities: 39 directly\nand 106 through named integrations. The other 20, including `parse/1`, remain\nuncredited. All 20 unsupported credits from the prior 165/165 claim are\nwithdrawn. Both C lanes record eight `parse/2` calls and zero `parse/1` calls.\nThe corrected counts and remaining arities are recorded in\n[`slice9_evidence.edn`](slice9_evidence.edn) and\n[`slice9_unexercised.edn`](slice9_unexercised.edn). The 20 earlier residual\nconsumers in [`fixture_closure.edn`](fixture_closure.edn) retain their concrete\nexpected outcomes; they do not establish closure of the full inventory.\n\n## Consumer evidence\n\n- `mkenv` preserves a nonempty global environment and creates empty locals.\n- `beta-red`, `delta-normalize`, and `delta-normalize-local` have explicit\n  entry fixtures and missing-reference rejections. The local wrapper also\n  exercises explicit local definitions and their precedence over an ambient\n  namespace definition.\n- `install-unfold-implicit!` installs into both isolated upstream atoms on A.\n  B/C return an updated immutable context, used by both the delta wrapper and\n  NBE. Both normalizers read the same installed extension. An installed\n  refusing extension propagates its original error data.\n- `install-opacity-function` starts from a refusing policy, explicitly installs\n  the working policy, then enters the original proof dispatcher through\n  opaque/transparent steps and qed. A duplicate opaque step rejects. A uses\n  an isolated namespace and host-local Var policy; B/C thread immutable\n  definition updates through the explicit context.\n- `install-fundamental-unparsers!` starts from an empty registry, installs the\n  fundamental product unparser, then unparses nested implications. Dependent\n  products and atoms remain unchanged. Direct `prod-impl-unparser` fixtures\n  also verify its `[term false]` refusal result.\n- `exists-kw?`, `term?`, `pair?`, and `vectorn?` include false results.\n  `bound-vars` distinguishes a bound occurrence from entirely free variables.\n  These fixtures preserve upstream behavior, including its shallow term\n  validator and the use of `vector` rather than `vector?` in utility predicates.\n- `proper-type?` enters each arity separately. Unknown variables throw;\n  a well-typed term whose inferred type is not a sort returns false.\n- `show-deftype` checks definition, axiom, theorem, implicit, and unknown\n  labels. It depends only on portable record-kind predicates and returns a\n  string. Host pprint and stdout functions remain excluded.\n- `safe-get` covers successful lookup, missing keys, stored false, and stored\n  nil. The latter three all reject upstream. `vremove` removes every match,\n  preserves a no-match vector, and propagates a predicate's domain exception.\n\n`mkenv`, `appify`, and `bound-vars` have no domain rejection in the pinned\nsource; the fixtures preserve their constructor, empty-input, and empty-set\nresults instead of manufacturing refusals. A refused product rewrite is\nidentified by its explicit modification flag, not mistaken for an exception.\n\nThe pinned local delta wrapper has a narrower portability scope than its\ndocstring suggests: `delta-step` drops `local?` when calling `delta-reduction`\non a reference. Consequently upstream can resolve a global or host namespace\ndefinition on that path. The global-result fixture preserves this behavior;\nthe namespace fixture supplies a local definition that shadows the ambient\none. The guest boundary still requires explicit definitions and has no host\nnamespace lookup capability. This round does not repair upstream or claim\nagreement for ambient-only resolution.\n\n## Acceptance counting\n\nEvery selected guest function body appends `[guest-function arity]` to an\nexplicit diagnostic stream at entry. Instrumentation covers all arities of\nthe functions supplied to each fixture, including higher-order calls through\nregistries and extension closures. Each C lane creates its own stream.\nAfter the VM halts, the harness reads that stream through its own\ncursor, preserving the fixture's original outcome and exception payload.\nA/B remain uninstrumented. Raw ordered call records are retained under\n`:c-walker :executed-calls` and `:c-semantic :executed-calls` for every completed\nfixture. The original uninstrumented fixtures used 1,000,000 instructions per\nlane. The instrumented fixtures have a 2,000,000 instruction bound because\nbody-entry stream writes add guest instructions. Reading the completed\ndiagnostic stream is harness work after halt. This is an instrumentation\nbudget change, not a new performance acceptance claim.\n\nPassing direct fixtures contribute only their original function/argument\ncount, and only with matching body-entry records in both C lanes. Passing\nnamed integrations contribute only exact arities recorded in both C lanes.\nStatic dependency membership contributes no credit. Full branch coverage is\nnot required. Context adapter identities and explicit extra context arguments\nare recorded in the evidence; each observed guest arity is checked against\nthat mapping individually, never against all arities of its function.\n\nOnly `:pass` fixture rows contribute exercise evidence. Recorded upstream\nfailures and deviations do not contribute. Any admitted arity without a\npassing direct or named integration execution path blocks the gate.\n\nThe accounting regression first failed against the old implementation: it\ncredited the deliberately uncalled arity and accepted missing C-lane traces.\nIt now requires both traces and leaves unused arities uncredited. A second\nregression replaces the adapted `parse/1` body in memory with an unconditional\nerror, then runs `:parse-to-normalize-to-type`. A/B and both C lanes still\npass; both C traces record `parse/2` and no `parse/1`, and the counter leaves\n`parse/1` uncredited. Every credited path is also checked against the recorded\nexecutions of its named fixture. See\n[`slice9_arity_accounting_test.txt`](slice9_arity_accounting_test.txt).\n\n## Recorded portability boundaries\n\nThe two `:recorded-deviation` rows remain `:elab-after-qed` and\n`:check-after-qed`. Upstream embeds `clojure.core/meta`, a host function, in\nexception data; C carries a guest closure. The original outcomes, comparison\nflags, exact payload guards, and evidence pointers remain intact. Both rows\nare listed in `:recorded-portability-boundaries` in the summary. Neither is\nreported as agreement.\n\n## Recorded upstream failures\n\n`:typing-ascription` still reaches the upstream declaration\n`type-of-ascribe`, which has no definition. A raises its original unbound-fn\nexception; B/C retain the explicit branch-local refusal.\n\n`:compile-malformed` still passes scalar proof input `[42]`. A/B raise the\noriginal native sequence exception; C retains its portable sequence refusal.\n\nThese two rows have the distinct disposition `:recorded-upstream-failure` and\nare listed in `:recorded-upstream-failures`. Their original A/B/C records and\nfalse agreement flags remain available. The disposition checks the exact\nfixture ID, input call, exception class/message, B outcome, and both completed\nC outcomes. Changed calls, IDs, or C payloads are tested to refuse the\ndisposition. Any other execution failure remains a blocker. No upstream fix,\naccepted-negative claim, or equality projection is introduced for these rows.\n\n## Verification\n\nThe accounting tests pass 2 tests and 22 assertions. An independent audit\nre-derives all 145 retained arities and all 360 credited fixture paths from\nthe two raw C traces. The four regression suites pass 122 tests and 1,338\nassertions. Kondo reports zero errors and warnings; cljstyle and\n`git diff --check` pass. The full oracle runs 26 tests and 1,879 assertions\nwith exactly one failure and no errors: its retained\n`(= :pass (:gate-status evidence))` closure assertion. The missing 20 arities\ntherefore continue to block acceptance. Verification results are recorded in\n[`slice9_verification.edn`](slice9_verification.edn), with command outputs\nbeside it.\n`yang.latte-oracle-test/write-kernel-gate-evidence!` explicitly regenerates\nboth execution evidence and the remaining-arity inventory; normal tests do\nnot write them. Evidence retains the pinned upstream revision, compiler\ndigest, runtime, instruction budget, per-fixture outcomes, diagnostic traces,\ndependency graph, admission inventory, credited paths, and all four recorded\nexception dispositions.\n\nThe append-only correction in [`known-gaps.md`](../../../docs/agents/known-gaps.md)\nrecords that host Clojure turns a thrown string into `ClassCastException`,\ncaught by `catch Exception` but not `catch ExceptionInfo`. Portable `try` binds\nthe error payload without equivalent runtime class handling; this guest/host\ngap remains declared.\n\nExecution scope is both C evaluators on the JVM. This correction does not\nclaim renewed architect sign-off or Node/Dart verification, and the blocked\ngate does not authorize program closure or merge.\n;; GENERATED from exact execution evidence in both C lanes.\n{[latte-kernel.defenv/axiom? 1] \"No passing direct fixture or named integration records this exact arity in both C lanes.\", [latte-kernel.defenv/definition? 1] \"No passing direct fixture or named integration records this exact arity in both C lanes.\", [latte-kernel.defenv/qualify-def 2] \"No passing direct fixture or named integration records this exact arity in both C lanes.\", [latte-kernel.defenv/registered-definition? 1] \"No passing direct fixture or named integration records this exact arity in both C lanes.\", [latte-kernel.defenv/registered-definition? 2] \"No passing direct fixture or named integration records this exact arity in both C lanes.\", [latte-kernel.defenv/registered-definition? 3] \"No passing direct fixture or named integration records this exact arity in both C lanes.\", [latte-kernel.nbe/evaluation 1] \"No passing direct fixture or named integration records this exact arity in both C lanes.\", [latte-kernel.nbe/instantiate-def 3] \"No passing direct fixture or named integration records this exact arity in both C lanes.\", [latte-kernel.norm/beta-reduction 1] \"No passing direct fixture or named int