jure: value-position adapters for native primitives cap at 4 arguments (magic constant), so a variadic primitive like assoc ([3 :variadic]) is refused with :arity at 5+ args though the primitive supports it; loud, not silent; architect disposition: ledger row, closing route = derive admitted counts from :yin.k/arities profiles, slice 7 (guest library) | slice 3b review (glm session 79d4df98, verified against vm.cljc:376-421) | yang.clojure | slice 7 | P3 |\n336:| yang.clojure: reverse interop is latent — a guest-convention fn value passed into a native/namespaced higher-order caller would be invoked with raw args (silent wrong on arity collision); no HOF primitive exists today; the corpus's one first-class use is (partial free-vars def-env); architect note: ledger now, closes when slice 5/7 apply/partial land | slice 3b review (glm session 79d4df98) | yang.clojure | slice 7 | P3 |\n337:| yang.clojure: three slice-4 residuals, bundled per architect disposition (fable 1793212600000): (1) a free REFERENCE to a reserved-prefix worker name (dsg__loop0 etc.) is not refused — binder enforcement covers bindings only, so user-typed (dsg__loop0 dsg__loop0 3) inside a 2-binding loop reaches the worker and nil-fills; closing route: a compile-program pre-pass refusing reserved-prefix symbols in any position; (2) bare recur/loop symbols in VALUE position compile as ordinary variables (host Clojure rejects unbound recur; binder-shadowed case matches Clojure); closing route: same pre-pass refusing recur/loop outside operator position; (3) the wrong-operand-count rejection message says \"loop bindings\" also for function-level targets (cosmetic; target records its kind) | slice 4 review (glm session 5ff7ec63, verified against compile-loop/clojure.cljc:879-955 and ast_walker.cljc zero :tail? references) | yang.clojure | slice 5 (all three: one pre-pass rides the outcome-channel work) | P3 |\n341:| yang.clojure: anonymous fn under the convention reports a nil name in its wrong-arity error data (lossy diagnostics only); closing route: carry a :yin.k/kind :anonymous marker plus the fn form's source metadata in the error data | slice 5 review (glm session in collab/1793242637826), architect disposition fable 8c1f0b7e | yang.clojure | slice 7 (first-class fns become common) | P3 |\n342:| yang.clojure: the and/or if-chain expansions bind host gensym names (and__/or__) outside the dsg__ reserved-prefix regime that slice 5 hardens; a colliding user-typed and__<n> would shadow inside the expansion; closing route: reserved-prefix names bound through the internal let | slice 5 review (glm session in collab/1793242637826; pre-existing at a34a2251) | yang.clojure | slice 6 (same expansion family) | P3 |\n343:| test-strategy: the throw-site table regeneration pokes a private test-ns function (throw-site-rows); only the failure message documents the procedure; closing route: a dev script beside src/dev/latte_oracle_manifest.clj that writes the table | slice 5 review (glm session in collab/1793242637826) | yang.clojure | slice 7 (next table-regenerating slice) | P3 |\n345:| yang.clojure: slice-6 case-arm fixtures are by constant KIND, not per corpus site — the plan's acceptance line asked for a minimal dispatch fixture for every arm of all six case sites (norm/proof keywords, mk-fresh numbers 0-3, typing symbols λ/Π); kinds are covered and mk-fresh runs end to end; closing route: derive fixtures from the corpus by walking the six sites, extracting each clause's constants, running a synthetic dispatch per arm+default (test-only addition) | slice 6 review (glm session 39b94aba), architect sign-off fable 8c1f0b7e | yang.clojure | slice 7 (test-only) | P3 |\n346:| yang.clojure: no cljs/cljd lane evidence exists for the slice-6 commits yet (JVM + kondo only at sign-off time); the outcome-adapter reader-conditional catch remains the one host-sensitive line on the branch | slice 6 review (glm session 39b94aba, fifth item); architect condition: lanes green at 7677c12b before merge | yang.clojure | slice-6 landing gate (this merge) | P3 |\n347:| yang.clojure: library `reduced?` matches any map holding :yin.k/reduced true (untyped tagged map, documented compromise in the library notes); closing route: a typed carrier if the VM gains one | slice 7 review (glm), fix round | yang.clojure | open | P3 |\n348:| yang.clojure: a `kind/*` or other qualified export in value position ignores its published arities (the adapter falls back to 0-4, so a bad count reaches the host); closing route: feed module profiles into the adapter counts alongside `:native` | slice 7 review (glm), fix round | yang.clojure | slice 8 | P3 |\n349:| yang.clojure: library-internal closures report the compiler's own source positions (:line/:column of the quoted library forms) in anonymous-fn arity errors; closing route: strip :line/:column metadata when building `library` | slice 7 review (glm), fix round | yang.clojure | slice 8 | P3 |\n350:| yang.clojure: `library-for` counts only `defn` as a program definition; a `def`/`defn-`/`defmacro` redefinition of a library name over-pulls the library definition (harmless, the program definition wins); closing route: share the compile-program defined-names scan | slice 7 review (glm), fix round | yang.clojure | slice 8 | P3 |\n351:| yang.clojure: `apply` surface gaps against Clojure, e.g. `(apply :k [{:k 1}])` fails loudly where Clojure returns 1; closing route: lower a keyword or collection operator in apply to the static lookup | slice 7 review (glm), fix round | yang.clojure | open | P3 |\n372:  `bb test:routed src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc` run selected 61 JVM namespaces:\n546:| yang.clojure: multi-arity `defn` and `fn` were deferred from slice 3 because a Yin closure nil-fills missing arguments and drops extras, so it cannot observe its call's argument count | architect session 8c1f0b7e (yang-slice3 sign-off), glm-5.3 review of yang-slice3 | yang.clojure | resolved-by-3b: opt-in argument-vector convention at `compile-program` (`:convention :arg-vector`), dispatch on `data/count` / `data/nth`, oracle legs `defenv/register-definition` (both arities, both placements) agree with upstream | P3 |\n547:| yang.clojure: destructuring lowers through the bare primitives `first`, `rest`, `empty?`; the compile-time refusal covers local bindings only, so a store `(def first ...)` or a runtime initial-`:env` entry named `first`/`rest`/`empty?` captures identically through `engine/resolve-var` (env, then store, then primitives) and is invisible to it | architect session 8c1f0b7e, glm-5.3 review of yang-slice3 | yang.clojure | open: a module-qualified alias for the core primitives (a VM-side registration question) or a refusal of top-level definitions of the three names | P3 |\n548:| yang.clojure: a destructured `&` remainder is a vector (the VM `rest` contract), where Clojure yields a seq or list; consumers that observe the shape (`list?`, `seq?`-sensitive printing) differ | architect session 8c1f0b7e ratified departure, `src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc` `nthnext-form` | yang.clojure | slice 7 (Clojure-compatible sequence operations) | P3 |\n549:| yang.clojure: variadic fn parameters (`[a & r]` at the top level of `fn` / `defn`) are rejected explicitly, also under the argument-vector convention | architect session 8c1f0b7e, corpus defines no variadic function | yang.clojure | slice 7 (the convention extends to variadic guest functions) | P3 |\n550:| yang.clojure: under :arg-vector, a store-level `(def conj ...)`/`(def get ...)` inside one program unit captures the convention's bare conj/get operators (resolve-var puts store ahead of primitives); engineer-verified: `(defn conj ([] [9 9]))` makes `(g 1 2)` return [9 9]; the compile-time locals check does not cover defs; architect disposition 1793206800000: ledger row, closing route = refuse top-level def of conj/get/first/rest/empty? under the convention in slice 5; cross-unit case covered by the store-shadowing row above | slice 3b review (glm session 79d4df98, verified against engine.cljc:129-132); architect sign-off fable 8c1f0b7e | yang.clojure | slice 5 (convention code touched there) | P3 |\n551:| yang.clojure: the ::defined scan in program-env reads only top-level heads, so a nested `(defn + ...)` inside do/if dispatches native while the store holds a convention closure — silent wrong result on the supported surface; architect disposition: ledger row, closing route = refuse non-top-level def under the convention, added when slice 4 registers loop/recur as special forms | slice 3b review (glm session 79d4df98, traced example (+ [1 2]) -> [:pair 1 2] not :one) | yang.clojure | slice 4 | P3 |\n552:| yang.clojure: defmacro parameters are the one binder surface skipping check-binder!/reserved-prefix validation (consciously deferred: the (defmacro defn [& xs]) test needs &); a reserved-prefix macro param can be shadowed by a generated cursor; macros never enter the convention so impact is body-level only; architect disposition: ledger row, closing route = &-aware per-param binder check, slice 4 with the nested-def refusal | slice 3b review (glm session 79d4df98) | yang.clojure | slice 4 | P3 |\n553:| yang.clojure: value-position adapters for native primitives cap at 4 arguments (magic constant), so a variadic primitive like assoc ([3 :variadic]) is refused with :arity at 5+ args though the primitive supports it; loud, not silent; architect disposition: ledger row, closing route = derive admitted counts from :yin.k/arities profiles, slice 7 (guest library) | slice 3b review (glm session 79d4df98, verified against vm.cljc:376-421) | yang.clojure | slice 7 | P3 |\n554:| yang.clojure: reverse interop is latent — a guest-convention fn value passed into a native/namespaced higher-order caller would be invoked with raw args (silent wrong on arity collision); no HOF primitive exists today; the corpus's one first-class use is (partial free-vars def-env); architect note: ledger now, closes when slice 5/7 apply/partial land | slice 3b review (glm session 79d4df98) | yang.clojure | slice 7 | P3 |\n555:| yang.clojure: three slice-4 residuals, bundled per architect disposition (fable 1793212600000): (1) a free REFERENCE to a reserved-prefix worker name (dsg__loop0 etc.) is not refused — binder enforcement covers bindings only, so user-typed (dsg__loop0 dsg__loop0 3) inside a 2-binding loop reaches the worker and nil-fills; closing route: a compile-program pre-pass refusing reserved-prefix symbols in any position; (2) bare recur/loop symbols in VALUE position compile as ordinary variables (host Clojure rejects unbound recur; binder-shadowed case matches Clojure); closing route: same pre-pass refusing recur/loop outside operator position; (3) the wrong-operand-count rejection message says \"loop bindings\" also for function-level targets (cosmetic; target records its kind) | slice 4 review (glm session 5ff7ec63, verified against compile-loop/clojure.cljc:879-955 and ast_walker.cljc zero :tail? references) | yang.clojure | slice 5 (all three: one pre-pass rides the outcome-channel work) | P3 |\n559:| yang.clojure: anonymous fn under the convention reports a nil name in its wrong-arity error data (lossy diagnostics only); closing route: carry a :yin.k/kind :anonymous marker plus the fn form's source metadata in the error data | slice 5 review (glm session in collab/1793242637826), architect disposition fable 8c1f0b7e | yang.clojure | slice 7 (first-class fns become common) | P3 |\n560:| yang.clojure: the and/or if-chain expansions bind host gensym names (and__/or__) outside the dsg__ reserved-prefix regime that slice 5 hardens; a colliding user-typed and__<n> would shadow inside the expansion; closing route: reserved-prefix names bound through the internal let | slice 5 review (glm session in collab/1793242637826; pre-existing at a34a2251) | yang.clojure | slice 6 (same expansion family) | P3 |\n561:| test-strategy: the throw-site table regeneration pokes a private test-ns function (throw-site-rows); only the failure message documents the procedure; closing route: a dev script beside src/dev/latte_oracle_manifest.clj that writes the table | slice 5 review (glm session in collab/1793242637826) | yang.clojure | slice 7 (next table-regenerating slice) | P3 |\n563:| yang.clojure: slice-6 case-arm fixtures are by constant KIND, not per corpus site — the plan's acceptance line asked for a minimal dispatch fixture for every arm of all six case sites (norm/proof keywords, mk-fresh numbers 0-3, typing symbols λ/Π); kinds are covered and mk-fresh runs end to end; closing route: derive fixtures from the corpus by walking the six sites, extracting each clause's constants, running a synthetic dispatch per arm+default (test-only addition) | slice 6 review (glm session 39b94aba), architect sign-off fable 8c1f0b7e | yang.clojure | slice 7 (test-only) | P3 |\n564:| yang.clojure: no cljs/cljd lane evidence exists for the slice-6 commits yet (JVM + kondo only at sign-off time); the outcome-adapter reader-conditional catch remains the one host-sensitive line on the branch | slice 6 review (glm session 39b94aba, fifth item); architect condition: lanes green at 7677c12b before merge | yang.clojure | slice-6 landing gate (this merge) | P3 |\n565:| yang.clojure: library `reduced?` matches any map holding :yin.k/reduced true (untyped tagged map, documented compromise in the library notes); closing route: a typed carrier if the VM gains one | slice 7 review (glm), fix round | yang.clojure | open | P3 |\n566:| yang.clojure: a `kind/*` or other qualified export in value position ignores its published arities (the adapter falls back to 0-4, so a bad count reaches the host); closing route: feed module profiles into the adapter counts alongside `:native` | slice 7 review (glm), fix round | yang.clojure | slice 8 | P3 |\n567:| yang.clojure: library-internal closures report the compiler's own source positions (:line/:column of the quoted library forms) in anonymous-fn arity errors; closing route: strip :line/:column metadata when building `library` | slice 7 review (glm), fix round | yang.clojure | slice 8 | P3 |\n568:| yang.clojure: `library-for` counts only `defn` as a program definition; a `def`/`defn-`/`defmacro` redefinition of a library name over-pulls the library definition (harmless, the program definition wins); closing route: share the compile-program defined-names scan | slice 7 review (glm), fix round | yang.clojure | slice 8 | P3 |\n569:| yang.clojure: `apply` surface gaps against Clojure, e.g. `(apply :k [{:k 1}])` fails loudly where Clojure returns 1; closing route: lower a keyword or collection operator in apply to the static lookup | slice 7 review (glm), fix round | yang.clojure | open | P3 |\n---\ndescription: Team roster, role routing, and reviewer-independence rules for datom.world\n---\n\n# DATOM.WORLD SOFTWARE ENGINEERING TEAM\n\nCanonical guide for the shared roster, role routing, and reviewer independence.\nThe Lead Engineering Orchestrator owns coordination, authorization, artifact\nprotocol, and verification in [`orchestrator.md`](./roles/orchestrator.md);\ndelegate session protocol and CLI recipes live in\n[`delegate-invocation-reference.md`](./delegate-invocation-reference.md).\n\n## Roster\n\nThe Orchestrator is deliberately absent from this table; it is the user-facing\nagent that centrally routes tasks to these roles. Each role name links to its\ndelegation prompt template under `docs/agents/roles/`. Review is one role\nbacked by one file, `roles/reviewer.md`, covering adversarial, routine, and\nsecurity-sign-off review via its Adversarial Review and Consensus Follow-up\ntemplates — adjust scope and rigor in the brief per flavor, not the file.\n\n+---------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Role                                              | Responsibility                                                                                         |\n+===================================================+========================================================================================================+\n| [Architect](roles/architect.md)                   | Axioms, invariants, boundaries, architecture                                                           |\n+---------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| [Compiler & AST](roles/compiler-engineer.md)      | AST, lowering, compile-time macros                                                                     |\n+---------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| [Frontend & Graphics](roles/graphics-engineer.md) | Events, WebGL/WebGPU, canvas, terminal                                                                 |\n+---------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| [QA & Verification](roles/qa-engineer.md)         | TDD, parity, lint, regression                                                                          |\n+---------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| [Review](roles/reviewer.md)                       | Adversarial defect discovery, routine correctness/portability checks, and security capability sign-off |\n+---------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| [Scoped / Subagent](roles/subagent.md)            | Bounded searches, edits, docs, lint                                                                    |\n+---------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| [Storage & Indexing](roles/storage-engineer.md)   | B-trees, indexing, DHT, storage, query                                                                 |\n+---------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| [Stream & Network](roles/stream-engineer.md)      | Streams, framing, concurrency, transports, codecs, RPC                                                 |\n+---------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| [VM Runtime](roles/vm-engineer.md)                | CESK, VMs, continuations, macros, loops                                                                |\n+---------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n\n\n## Available Subscriptions & Cost Constraints\n\nThe Orchestrator routes work using these cost profiles. Flat subscriptions are preferred; metered models are reserved for tasks explicitly requiring them.\n\n+-----------------+----------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Provider        | Cost Structure                         | Routing Notes                                                                                               |\n+=================+========================================+=============================================================================================================+\n| agy             | Flat: Included                         | Treated as a flat subscription. Use freely within budget.                                                   |\n+-----------------+----------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| claude          | Inactive / Cancelled                   | Subscription stopped by owner. Do not route tasks here.                                                     |\n+-----------------+----------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| claude-api      | Inactive / Cancelled                   | Anthropic API access stopped by owner. Do not route tasks here.                                             |\n+-----------------+----------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| codex           | Flat: ChatGPT Plus ($20/mo)            | Strict rate limits. Available for any role (architecture, implementation, review, QA).                      |\n|                 |                                        | All codex models (astra, sol, terra, luna) draw ONE shared pool: a cut darkens every seat at once, so       |\n|                 |                                        | sequence codex dispatches rather than fanning them out. Independent parallel capacity is codex | kimi | glm.|\n+-----------------+----------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| deepseek        | Metered: Pay-by-token                  | Flash: $0.14/M in, $0.28/M out. Pro: $0.435/M in, $0.87/M out.                                              |\n|                 |                                        | Off-peak (10:00-01:00, 04:00-06:00 UTC & wknds) is 50% cheaper.                                             |\n|                 |                                        | Cache hits are ~50x cheaper. Reserve for necessary work.                                                    |\n+-----------------+----------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| glm             | Flat: Pro yearly plan ($672/yr)        | Peak hours: weekdays 14:00-18:00 UTC+8. Schedule large jobs off-peak (50% rate).                            |\n+-----------------+----------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| kimi            | Flat: Kimi membership (Plus+)          | Invoke only through the dedicated `kimi` CLI. Monthly quotas apply.                                          |\n|                 |                                        | Pro unlocks Highspeed and 1M context for K3.x.                                                               |\n+-----------------+----------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| mistral         | Flat: Mistral Pro ($20/mo)             | Invoke via `vibe` CLI. Model aliases: mistral-large, mistral-medium-3.5, mistral-4.0, devstral, codestral.   |\n+-----------------+----------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| muse            | Meta login / `META_API_KEY`            | Invoke via `muse` CLI; headless form is `muse exec`. Models:                                                |\n|                 |                                        | `muse-wonder-1.0` (flagship) and `muse-spark-1.3` (fast lane).                                              |\n|                 |                                        | Check [`routing-status.md`](./routing-status.md) before dispatch (geo-restricted at last relay).            |\n+-----------------+----------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n\n## Model Strengths and Selection Guide\n\nThe following guide details the strengths, weaknesses, and optimal use cases for each model based on their current (late 2026) technical capabilities.\n\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Model                       | Strengths & When to Use                                            | Weaknesses & When Not to Use                                        |\n+=============================+====================================================================+=====================================================================+\n| `claude-fable-5-1`          | Highest capability for long-horizon agentic work, complex research,| Slower inference; resource hog (rate limits); mandatory             |\n|                             | strict boundaries, and managing vast context (efficient caching).  | watermarking. **Avoid for:** Routine fast-loop tasks; precise       |\n|                             | **Best for:** Architecture definition, security sign-offs.         | one-shot implementations where strict rigor is overkill.            |\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `claude-opus-5.5`           | Performs at Fable 5.1 levels for most work at markedly lower cost  | Still higher latency/cost than Sonnet 5. **Avoid for:** Fast QA     |\n|                             | than the prior Opus generation. **Best for:** Code migration,      | loops or simple code generation where Sonnet 5 suffices.            |\n|                             | software optimization, and complex agentic coding.                 |                                                                     |\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `claude-sonnet-5-5`         | Frontier agentic workhorse; substantial gains in reasoning depth,  | Can struggle with high-order axiomatic proofs or subtle security    |\n|                             | instruction following, and rigorous code audits. Low latency and    | capability proofs vs Fable 5.1 / Astra. **Avoid for:** Final        |\n|                             | high cost-efficiency. **Best for:** Core implementation, deep QA   | architectural sign-offs on high-risk cryptographic or consensus     |\n|                             | adversarial code reviews, multi-file refactors, and test suites.   | boundaries where Opus 5.5 / Fable 5.1 / Astra is mandated.          |\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `deepseek-flash`            | V4.1-Flash outpaces V4-Pro in speed, cost, and agentic benchmarks. | Can hallucinate on deep invariant constraints. **Avoid for:**       |\n|                             | **Best for:** QA parity checks, scoped subagent work, and          | Complex system design, AST lowering.                                |\n|                             | high-efficiency native multimodal tasks.                           |                                                                     |\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `deepseek-v4-pro`           | Legacy 1.6T MoE architecture. **Best for:** Advanced logic         | Actively being superseded by V4.1-Flash; high cost and undercooked  |\n|                             | workflows and adversarial reviews.                                 | relative to size. **Avoid for:** General use (use Flash instead).   |\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `gemini-3.8-flash`          | Lightning-fast; highly cost-efficient; 1M+ context with native     | Can oversimplify subtle invariant edge cases vs frontier models.    |\n|                             | multimodal support. **Best for:** High-volume agentic loops,       | **Avoid for:** Core security capability enforcement; top-level      |\n|                             | visual/frontend inspection, rapid TDD/QA, and subagents.           | architecture.                                                       |\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `gemini-4-argon`            | Frontier reasoning with configurable effort and deep synthesis     | Higher latency/cost at medium/high effort than Flash-tier models;   |\n|                             | across large multi-file specs and codebases; strong architectural  | sandboxed headless CLI cannot run local JVM (`clojure`) suites.     |\n|                             | judgment, invariant verification, and surgical cross-checking.     | **Avoid for:** High-volume trivial edits, mechanical sweeps, or     |\n|                             | **Best for:** System architecture, design/code review, complex     | headless tasks requiring in-session JVM test execution.             |\n|                             | debugging, and multi-step implementation.                          |                                                                     |\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `glm-5.3`                   | Scaled post-training for exceptional long-horizon tasks and        | Heavier footprint; can overcomplicate simple data framing. **Avoid  |\n|                             | cybersecurity. **Best for:** Complex programming workflows, VMs,   | for:** UI/UX, canvas, or WebGPU tasks.                              |\n|                             | storage, indexing, DHTs, and networking.                           |                                                                     |\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `glm-5.3-flash`             | High performance at a lower computational cost; steep discounts    | Limited complex reasoning on novel architectures; self-assessment   |\n|                             | off-peak and weekend boosts. Excels when claims are checkable      | is unreliable — judge it behaviorally. **Avoid for:** Core          |\n|                             | against an authority: evidence gathering, citation verification,   | architecture, unbounded design authorship, peak-hour execution if   |\n|                             | reconciliation, protocol discipline. **Best for:** Scoped          | budget is tight.                                                    |\n|                             | subagents, bounded searches, QA spot-checks, verification seats.   |                                                                     |\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `gpt-5.4-mini`              | Fast, lower-cost model for bounded, repetitive work with explicit  | Do not use as the sole judge of subtle invariants or broad design.  |\n|                             | acceptance criteria. **Best for:** TDD loops, simple linting,      | Escalate multi-step refactors and disputed findings to Terra, Sol,  |\n|                             | extraction, and scoped text edits.                                 | or Astra.                                                           |\n| `gpt-6-astra`               | Most capable end-to-end model. **Best for:** Hardest architecture, | Highest cost. Reserve for the highest-risk or most difficult work   |\n|                             | security, research, coding, computer-use, and long-horizon tasks.  | under the subscription policy.                                      |\n|                             | Capability includes implementation across any role.                |                                                                     |\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `gpt-6-luna`                | Most affordable GPT-6 model; optimized for high-volume tasks and   | Low-cost does not mean suitable for unbounded design or the hardest |\n|                             | fast response times. Improved factuality and coding. **Best for:** | invariant reasoning. Escalate complex architecture/security.        |\n|                             | High-volume tasks, extraction, scoped searches, and routine QA.    |                                                                     |\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `gpt-6.1-sol`               | Intelligent everyday workhorse with near-Astra intelligence, 1M+   | More expensive than Luna. Use Astra when the task is the hardest    |\n|                             | context window, and 95% prompt cache discount. Upgraded reasoning  | end-to-end problem requiring frontier architectural verification.   |\n|                             | effort control (low to max), +6.4% DeepSWE / +7% OSWorld gains over|                                                                     |\n|                             | predecessor. **Best for:** Complex implementation, advanced coding,|                                                                     |\n|                             | high-stakes security, and architectural review.                    |                                                                     |\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `gpt-6.1-terra`             | Balanced intelligence and cost. **Best for:** Default production   | Use Sol or Astra for the hardest reasoning, high-risk security, or  |\n|                             | engineering, integration work, network streams, VM runtimes,       | architecture decisions. Validate compiler-lowering and other        |\n|                             | moderate-complexity implementation, and subagent fallback.         | specialist work with focused tests and independent review.          |\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `kimi-for-coding-highspeed` | K2.7 Code Highspeed; 5-6x faster output with 256K context.         | Uses about 3x quota; fixed thinking; Pro tier required. **Avoid     |\n|                             | **Best for:** Latency-sensitive routine coding and iteration.      | for:** 1M-context work or tasks needing adjustable reasoning.       |\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `k3.1-pro`                  | Latest 3T-class flagship; 1M context, image/video input, and       | Higher resource use than K3 256K; thinking is mandatory; 1M         |\n|                             | strongest long-horizon agentic coding. **Best for:** Hardest       | context requires Pro. **Avoid for:** Routine Clojure or latency-    |\n|                             | implementation, large refactors, architecture, deep review, and    | sensitive loops; use K3 256K or Highspeed instead. Also avoid       |\n|                             | difficult multi-file Clojure, compiler, VM, and invariant work.    | clients that cannot keep thinking enabled.                          |\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `k3.1-pro-highspeed`        | Same K3.1 Pro capabilities with 2-3x faster output and 1M          | About 2x quota usage; Pro tier; accelerates model output, not       |\n|                             | context. **Best for:** Responsive complex coding, long-horizon     | tools. **Avoid for:** Quota-sensitive or tool-bound work where      |\n|                             | agents, and iterative difficult debugging.                         | regular K3.1 Pro has the same quality at lower quota cost.          |\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `k3`                        | Previous 2.8T flagship; 1M context, image/video input, adjustable  | Superseded by K3.1 Pro; 1M uses about 2x the quota of K3 256K       |\n|                             | thinking. **Best for:** Large-codebase analysis, multi-file        | and requires Pro. **Avoid for:** Hardest tasks when K3.1 Pro is     |\n|                             | refactors, and long-document processing.                           | available, or small jobs that fit K3 256K.                          |\n+-----------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------