{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"command -v bb; sed -n '1740,1990p' test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj; sed -n '570,590p' docs/agents/known-gaps.md; sed -n '3514,3555p' docs/design/yang.antlr.md; sed -n '1,85p' docs/agents/team.md; cat test/yang/latte_oracle/slice9_verification.edn\"","aggregated_output":"/Users/sto/.local/share/mise/installs/babashka/1.13.225/bb\n\n\n(def ^:private kernel-context-integration-names\n  ;; Integrations also enter existing explicit-context adapters directly,\n  ;; rather than their gate-* wrappers. Keep those identities explicit.\n  {'latte-kernel.defenv/local-definitions 'local-definitions\n   'latte-kernel.defenv/register-definition 'register-definition\n   'latte-kernel.proof/is-opaque? 'is-opaque?\n   'latte-kernel.unparser/unregister-unparser! 'unregister-unparser\n   'latte-kernel.unparser/unparse 'unparse\n   'latte-kernel.unparser/register-unparser! 'register-unparser\n   'latte-kernel.unparser/unparse-term-once 'unparse-term-once\n   'latte-kernel.unparser/unparse-term-rec 'unparse-term-rec\n   'latte-kernel.norm/install-unfold-implicit! 'install-unfold-implicit\n   'latte-kernel.unparser/install-fundamental-unparsers! 'install-fundamental-unparsers\n   'latte-kernel.unparser/prod-impl-unparser 'prod-impl-unparser})\n\n\n(def ^:private kernel-context-extra-arguments\n  ;; Immutable-context APIs take one explicit context where upstream reads\n  ;; or writes an ambient binding. Raw guest arities remain in the trace.\n  {'unregister-unparser 1 'unparse 1 'register-unparser 1\n   'install-unfold-implicit 1 'install-fundamental-unparsers 1\n   'gate-proof-install-opacity-function 1})\n\n\n(defn- kernel-trace-form\n  \"Append the selected function/arity at body entry, including indirect calls.\n   Only C is instrumented; A/B still run their original fixture paths.\"\n  [form]\n  (if (#{'defn 'defn-} (first form))\n    (let [[op fname & header] form\n          header (drop-while #(not (or (vector? %) (seq? %))) header)\n          trace-arity (fn [[params & body]]\n                        (let [n (if (some #{'&} params)\n                                  [:variadic (.indexOf params '&)] (count params))]\n                          (list* params\n                                 (list 'stream/put! 'gate-execution-trace (list 'quote [fname n]))\n                                 body)))]\n      (list* op fname (if (vector? (first header))\n                        (trace-arity header) (map trace-arity header))))\n    form))\n\n\n(defn- kernel-executed-arity?\n  [row symbol n]\n  (let [names (remove nil? [(kernel-guest-name symbol)\n                            (get kernel-context-integration-names symbol)])\n        paths (set (for [fname names]\n                     [fname (if (number? n)\n                              (+ n (get kernel-context-extra-arguments fname 0)) n)]))]\n    (every? (fn [lane] (some paths (get-in row [lane :executed-calls])))\n            [:c-walker :c-semantic])))\n\n\n(defn- kernel-compile\n  [fixture]\n  (let [call (kernel-c-program fixture)\n        forms (kernel-closure call)\n        program (into ['(def gate-execution-trace (stream/make 1000000))]\n                      (concat (map kernel-trace-form forms) [call]))\n        program (into (yang/library-for program) program)\n        opts (update arg-vector-opts :native into\n                     (map (fn [[op profile]] [(clojure.core/symbol \"stream\" (name op)) profile])\n                          module/stream-profiles))\n        ast (yang/compile-program program true opts)\n        missing (unbound-ast-symbols ast program)]\n    (if (seq missing)\n      {:status :blocked :phase :admission :blocked-by (vec (sort missing))\n       :owners (into {} (for [s missing]\n                          [s (mapv second (filter #(some #{s} (tree-seq coll? seq %)) program))]))}\n      {:status :compiled :ast ast :forms forms})))\n\n\n(defn- kernel-c\n  [compiled lane]\n  (if (= :compiled (:status compiled))\n    (assoc (kernel-attempt\n             #(let [machine (compiler-test/run-guest-machine\n                              (:ast compiled) (oracle-registry yang/kind-module) lane 2000000)\n                    v (vm/value machine)\n                    ref (get-in machine [:store 'gate-execution-trace])\n                    handle (get-in machine [:resources (:id ref)])]\n                (when-not (and (vm/halted? machine) handle)\n                  (throw (ex-info \"Execution trace did not complete\" {:lane lane})))\n                {:status :completed :outcome (evidence-data v) :shape (result-shape (evidence-data v))\n                 :executed-calls (tu/drain handle)}))\n           :lane lane)\n    (assoc compiled :lane lane)))\n\n\n(deftest test-kernel-proof-fresh-supply\n  (testing \"explicit have types advance the supply; inferred types and prior contexts are preserved\"\n    (doseq [[name next-name] [['h 'h2] ['_ '_]]\n            [ty allocations] [['A 1] ['_ 0]]]\n      (let [fixture\n            {:call\n             (walk/postwalk-replace\n               {'H name 'H2 next-name 'TY ty}\n               '(let [k0 env\n                      [s1 [k1 ctx1 deps1 uses1]]\n                      (latte-kernel.proof/elab-have k0 '[[A ✳] [x A]] [] {} 'H 'TY 'x {})\n                      [s2 [k2 ctx2 deps2 uses2]]\n                      (latte-kernel.proof/elab-have k1 ctx1 deps1 uses1 'H2 'TY 'x {})\n                      [s3 [k3 ctx3 deps3 uses3]]\n                      (latte-kernel.proof/elab-declare k2 ctx2 deps2 uses2 'z 'A {})\n                      [s4 [k4 ctx4 deps4 uses4]]\n                      (latte-kernel.proof/elab-have k3 ctx3 deps3 uses3 '_ 'A 'z {})]\n                  [[s1 s2 s3 s4]\n                   [(:fresh k0) (:fresh k1) (:fresh k2) (:fresh k3) (:fresh k4)]\n                   (mapv (fn [k] (count (second (:definitions k)))) [k0 k1 k2 k3 k4])\n                   (= ctx1 '[[A ✳] [x A]]) (= ctx1 ctx2)]))}\n            expected [[:ok :ok :ok :ok] [0 allocations (* 2 allocations)\n                                         (inc (* 2 allocations)) (+ 2 (* 2 allocations))]\n                      (if (= name '_) [0 0 0 0 0] [0 1 2 2 2])\n                      true true]\n            compiled (kernel-compile fixture)]\n        (is (= {:value expected} (:outcome (kernel-b fixture))))\n        (doseq [lane [:walker :semantic]]\n          (let [c (kernel-c compiled lane)]\n            (is (= [:yin.k/ok expected] (:outcome c)) (str name \" \" lane \" \" c)))))))\n  (testing \"the proof loop carries the returned supply to the next step and qed\"\n    (doseq [[script fresh]\n            [['((:have h A x {}) (:have h2 A x {}) (:qed x {})) 2]\n             ['((:have _ A x {}) (:have _ A x {}) (:qed x {})) 2]\n             ['((:declare z A {}) (:have h A z {}) (:declare w A {}) (:have h2 A w {}) (:qed w {})) 4]]]\n      (let [fixture {:call (list 'let ['r (list 'latte-kernel.proof/elab-proof 'env ''[[A ✳] [x A]]\n                                                (list 'quote script))]\n                                 '[(first r) (:fresh (first (second r))) (:fresh env) (:definitions env)])}\n            expected [:ok fresh 0 [{} {}]]\n            compiled (kernel-compile fixture)]\n        (is (= {:value expected} (:outcome (kernel-b fixture))))\n        (doseq [lane [:walker :semantic]]\n          (is (= [:yin.k/ok expected] (:outcome (kernel-c compiled lane))) (str lane))))))\n  (testing \"check-proof keeps the rebuilt context under its own def-env' binding\"\n    ;; Observe the context passed to the final comparison: check-proof's public\n    ;; result contains only the proof term and type, so it cannot expose :fresh.\n    (with-redefs [kernel-sources\n                  (assoc kernel-sources 'gate-norm-beta-eq?\n                         '(defn gate-norm-beta-eq?\n                            [def-env ctx t1 t2]\n                            (= 1 (:fresh def-env))))]\n      (let [fixture {:call '(latte-kernel.proof/check-proof env '[[A ✳] [x A]] 't 'A '[[:qed x {}]])}\n            expected [:ok ['x 'A]]\n            compiled (kernel-compile fixture)]\n        (is (= {:value expected} (:outcome (kernel-b fixture))))\n        (doseq [lane [:walker :semantic]]\n          (is (= [:yin.k/ok expected] (:outcome (kernel-c compiled lane))) (str lane)))))))\n\n\n(def ^:private kernel-named-integrations\n  ;; Only these named integrations may contribute observed transitive calls.\n  ;; Static dependencies never establish execution of any arity.\n  #{:unparser-guest-closure :implicit-typed-closure :implicit-throwing-closure\n    :check-assume-have-discharge :parse-to-normalize-to-type\n    :fundamental-install-use :unfold-install-use :opacity-install-use})\n\n\n(def ^:private kernel-unexercised-reasons\n  (edn/read-string (slurp (io/resource \"yang/latte_oracle/slice9_unexercised.edn\"))))\n\n\n(defn- kernel-exercise-entries\n  [inventory fixtures results]\n  (vec\n    (for [{:keys [symbol arities disposition]} inventory\n          :when (= :admitted disposition)\n          n arities\n          :let [direct (vec (for [[fixture row] (map vector fixtures results)\n                                  :when (and (= :pass (:status row))\n                                             (kernel-executed-arity? row symbol n)\n                                             (= symbol (first (:call fixture)))\n                                             (= n (count (rest (:call fixture)))))]\n                              (:id fixture)))\n                integration (vec (for [row results\n                                       :when (and (= :pass (:status row))\n                                                  (contains? kernel-named-integrations (:id row))\n                                                  (kernel-executed-arity? row symbol n))]\n                                   (:id row)))]]\n      {:function-arity [symbol n]\n       :exercise (cond (seq direct) :direct\n                       (seq integration) :named-integration\n                       :else :unexercised)\n       :direct-fixture-ids direct\n       :integration-fixture-ids integration\n       :missing-dependency (when (and (empty? direct) (empty? integration))\n                             (get kernel-unexercised-reasons [symbol n]\n                                  \"No passing direct fixture or named integration records this exact arity in both C lanes.\"))})))\n\n\n(defn- kernel-host-value-deviation\n  [id a b walker semantic]\n  ;; Disposition only: keep every original comparison and all four outcomes.\n  ;; A new failure outside this exact host-value boundary remains a mismatch.\n  (when (and (contains? #{:elab-after-qed :check-after-qed} id)\n             (= :completed (:status a) (:status b) (:status walker) (:status semantic))\n             (= (:outcome a) (:outcome b))\n             (= (get-in a [:outcome :thrown :data :meta])\n                {:nonportable-host-value (str (class clojure.core/meta))})\n             (= \"Wrong proof script: proof steps after qed.\"\n                (get-in a [:outcome :thrown :message]))\n             (every? (fn [c]\n                       (and (= :yin.k/error (first (:outcome c)))\n                            (= (get-in a [:outcome :thrown :message])\n                               (get-in c [:outcome 1 :yin.k/message]))\n                            (= (dissoc (get-in a [:outcome :thrown :data]) :meta)\n                               (dissoc (get-in c [:outcome 1 :yin.k/data]) :meta))\n                            (= (result-shape (dissoc (get-in a [:outcome :thrown :data]) :meta))\n                               (result-shape (dissoc (get-in c [:outcome 1 :yin.k/data]) :meta)))\n                            (values/closure? (get-in c [:outcome 1 :yin.k/data :meta]))))\n                     [walker semantic]))\n    {:boundary :nonportable-host-value\n     :upstream-value 'clojure.core/meta\n     :evidence {:file \"slice9_evidence.edn\" :fixture-id id\n                :path [:a :outcome :thrown :data :meta]}\n     :reason \"Upstream embeds a host function in error data; the guest carries its closure. No agreement is claimed.\"}))\n\n\n(defn- kernel-upstream-failure\n  \"Disposition for two pinned upstream failures, guarded by the original call\n   and every observed boundary. A new failure must still block the gate.\"\n  [fixture a b walker semantic]\n  (let [completed-c? (= :completed (:status walker) (:status semantic))\n        c (:outcome walker)\n        same-c? (= c (:outcome semantic))\n        unbound-message \"Pinned upstream declaration has no definition\"\n        unbound-data {:refusal :upstream-unbound :binding 'latte-kernel.typing/type-of-ascribe}\n        failure\n        (when (and completed-c? same-c?)\n          (case (:id fixture)\n            :typing-ascription\n            (when (and (= (:call fixture)\n                          '(latte-kernel.typing/type-of-term env '[[A ✳] [x A]]\n                                                             '(:latte-kernel.syntax/ascribe x A)))\n                       (= :blocked (:status a))\n                       (= \"class java.lang.IllegalStateException\" (:exception a))\n                       (= \"Attempting to call unbound fn: #'latte-kernel.typing/type-of-ascribe\" (:message a))\n                       (= :completed (:status b))\n                       (= {:thrown {:message unbound-message :data unbound-data}} (:outcome b))\n                       (= [:yin.k/error {:yin.k/message unbound-message :yin.k/data unbound-data}] c))\n              {:boundary :upstream-unbound-definition\n               :reason \"Pinned upstream declares type-of-ascribe without a definition; B/C explicitly refuse that branch.\"})\n            :compile-malformed\n            (when (and (= (:call fixture) '(latte-kernel.proof/compile-proof '[42]))\n                       (= :blocked (:status a) (:status b))\n                       (= \"class java.lang.IllegalArgumentException\" (:exception a) (:exception b))\n                       (= \"Don't know how to create ISeq from: java.lang.Long\" (:message a) (:message b))\n                       (nil? (:data a)) (nil? (:data b))\n                       (= [:yin.k/error {:yin.k/message \"seq of a non-collection is not supported\"\n                                         :yin.k/data {:value 42}}] c))\n              {:boundary :upstream-malformed-proof-sequence\n| jolt ws: a ping after a local close is queued gets no pong (A4 \"every ping produces one pong\" holds until a local close exists) | pump.jolt:597 (handle-event! :ping guard); wave-1 Architect sign-off P3 | jolt (J6) | documented behaviour, closed by J6e's docstring sentences; J6h cites the archived sign-off | P3 |\n| yang.php: compile-time default validation is incomplete: `literal-fits?` handles only string, int, bool and null literals; a float default for an `int` property or parameter (`public int $x = 1.5`, `function f(int $x = 1.5)`), arrays and signed literals pass lowering, and `:unknown` is returned whenever a class, object, callable or iterable member exists, so plainly invalid scalar defaults escape; PHP rejects resolved defaults during compilation | P4 S5 independent review (codex gpt-6.1-sol round 2, P2) and Architect sign-off condition 5; `src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc` default checks | yang.php | P4 S6 (classify statically evaluable defaults completely; defer only genuinely unresolved expressions) | P2 |\n| yang.php: a later rejected by-reference argument leaks an earlier parameter's ownership: with `function f(D &$a, int &$b)`, `$a = new D`, `$b = 'bad'`, catching `f($a, $b)` and then `unset($a)` destroys the object after the next statement (`cED`) instead of before it (`cDE`), because cleanup surrounds only the body and not parameter initialization | P4 S5 independent review (codex gpt-6.1-sol round 2, P2) and Architect sign-off condition 5; `src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc` parameter initialization | yang.php | P4 S6 (cover parameter initialization in the unwinding cleanup so every acquired parameter is released on failure) | P2 |\n| yang.php: a later rejected by-reference argument leaks an earlier parameter's acquisition: with `function f(D &$a, int &$b)`, `$a = new D`, `$b = 'bad'`, `catch (TypeError $e)`, then `$e = null; unset($a); echo 'E';`, PHP prints `cDE` (the freed trace releases its copy, `unset` drops the last holder) and yang.php prints `cED` (the acquisition of `$a` made before `$b` failed is never released, because cleanup surrounds only the body, not parameter initialization). Without `$e = null` both print `cED`, PHP because the trace holds the argument (corner C30), yang.php because of this leak | P4 S5 independent review (codex gpt-6.1-sol round 2, P2) and Architect sign-off condition 5, restated by the 17.3 ruling of 2026-10-28; `src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc` parameter initialization | yang.php | P4 S6 (cover parameter initialization in the unwinding cleanup so every acquired parameter is released on failure); fixture `:decl-byref-later-rejection-releases-earlier-param` (`#{:p4-s6}`) | P2 |\n| yang.php C30: exception traces hold no arguments, so an argument rejected at acquisition is released at the throw where PHP keeps it alive until the exception is freed (departure `cDE` vs `cED`) | 17.3 ruling 2026-10-28 on oracle pass 9; design section 17.2 C30 | yang.php | none scheduled; a trace-frames slice | P3 |\n| yang.php: compile-time default validation was incomplete (a float default for an `int` property or parameter, arrays and signed literals passed lowering, and `:unknown` escaped whenever a class, object, callable or iterable member existed); closed in P4 S6: `init-kind` classifies int, float, string, bool, null, array and signed numeric defaults from the syntax tree, `kind-fits?` holds them to the declared type (an int widens to float; callable members accept strings and arrays), and only a default that names a constant is checked when the object or parameter is installed | P4 S5 independent review (codex gpt-6.1-sol round 2, P2) and Architect sign-off condition 5; `src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc` default checks | yang.php | closed in P4 S6; `compile-errors-are-named-test` entries and fixture `:decl-signed-literal-defaults-accepted` | P2 |\n| yang.php: a later rejected by-reference argument leaked an earlier parameter's acquisition (`function f(D &$a, int &$b)`, `$a = new D`, `$b = 'bad'`: PHP prints `cDE` once the trace is freed, yang.php printed `cED`); closed in P4 S6: declared parameter types are checked in the body's prologue (`php/check-param-cell!`), inside the cleanup that releases every parameter cell, and a function with a typed parameter always takes the unwinding cleanup path. Without freeing the exception both print `cED` (corner C30) | P4 S5 independent review (codex gpt-6.1-sol round 2, P2) and Architect sign-off condition 5, restated by the 17.3 ruling of 2026-10-28; `src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc` parameter initialization | yang.php | closed in P4 S6; fixture `:decl-byref-later-rejection-releases-earlier-param` (enabled) | P2 |\n| yang.php: first-class callable syntax (`strlen(...)`, `$o->m(...)`, `A::m(...)`) is a syntax error in the vendored `PhpParser.g4` (an argument list has no bare `...`); `Closure::fromCallable` and the callable forms (`[$o, 'm']`, `'A::m'`, invokable objects) carry the semantics | P4 S6; `antlr/php/PhpParser.g4` `arguments` | yang.php | a grammar patch slice before P4 S10 (php-src selection uses the syntax) | P3 |\n| yang.php: the `callable` type check (`php/callable?`) sees only public methods, because a type check has no calling scope; a private `[$this, 'm']` callable passed to a `callable` parameter from inside its class is rejected, while calling it directly works; closure rebinding does not warn for the unbind-`$this` and internal-class cases, and `static` is accepted only as a return type (as a parameter or property type it never matches) | P4 S6 | yang.php | P4 S10 (departure fixtures from the object permutation alphabet) | P3 |\n| yang.php: destructuring by reference into a property (`[&$o->p] = $a`, row 47) is not lowered: the destructuring pattern accepts variable and element targets only; fixture `:obj-row47-destructure-by-ref-into-typed-property` is authored and gated by `:p4-row47` | checkpoint B audit (fixture package); `src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc` `pattern-of` | yang.php | P4 S7 or a destructuring slice before checkpoint C (the gate lifts when the pattern accepts property targets) | P2 |\n| yang.php: `exit`/`die` is refused (`:constant`), so row 51 (`exit` inside a destructor proceeds to shutdown, corner C9) cannot run; fixture `:obj-row51-exit-in-destructor` is authored and gated by `:p4-exit` | checkpoint B audit (fixture package); design row 51 | yang.php | P4 S7 (the exit escape and the pending-exception destruct halt) | P2 |\n| yang.php: a direct write to a typed static property (`A::$s = 'x'`) is not type-checked; only a reference to it is (a source keyed by class and property, row 46), so assignment through the reference is checked and the direct assignment is not | checkpoint B audit (fixture package) | yang.php | P4 S10 (departure fixture from the object permutation alphabet) or earlier if the sprop write path gains a coercion | P3 |\n| yang.php C37: `__FILE__`/`__DIR__` use the unit's declared relative path (`.` for a root unit), not absolute filesystem paths; fixture `:const-file-dir-raw` records the departure and oracle comparison normalizes `/w/<id>` to `.` | P6 plan v2 R2, design 17.2 C37 | yang.php | retained as a P6 departure | P3 |\n| yang.php C38: folded include paths resolve at the composition root; PHP's including-script directory and additional `include_path` searches are not modelled | P6 plan v2 R3, design 17.2 C38 | yang.php | composition supplies a literal path map; resolution expansion is a later inclusion slice | P3 |\n| yang.php C39: `spl_autoload_register(null)` and implicit Composer filesystem resolution are refused; literal Composer class-map data is supported | P6 plan v2 R9, design 17.2 C39 | yang.php | retained as a P6 departure; fixture `default-autoloader-is-refused-test` | P3 |\n### 8.10 Shared function calling convention\n\n**Status: proposed, 2026-10-29; not implemented.** This section specifies\none invocation convention for every conforming ANTLR-based Yang frontend.\nExamples are non-exhaustive. Adding a language must not add a case to Yang\ncore, a language-pair bridge, or an evaluator transition. The words\n**must**, **must not**, and **may** state requirements on a future\nconforming implementation, not claims about current code.\n\n#### 8.10.1 Decision and scope\n\nA Yin function is a Yin function, independent of its source syntax. The\nVM applies a closure to operands. Source calling semantics are ordinary\nUAST implementing the call site and callee entry, above the VM.\n\nEvery supported source-level callable, after its source-required\nresolution, exposes `:yang.call/entry`, an ordinary Yin closure taking\nexactly one invocation record. Invoking it is an ordinary\n`:application`. No AST tag, lambda slot, instruction, host primitive,\neffect kind, or source-language test is added to the VM.\n\nThis is an internal calling convention, not a transport, another system\nABI, or a host callback interface. Calls between source languages inside\none task do not themselves cross a host boundary. Portable I/O and\ncross-task interaction continue through `dao.stream`.\n\n**Transparent calling** means that an admitted callable can be passed,\nstored, returned, and invoked by any conforming frontend without a\nhandwritten language-pair wrapper. It does not mean every foreign value\nsatisfies every source operation. Invocation, name visibility, and value\ninterpretation are distinct obligations.\n\nThe convention covers ordinary source calls and supported callable\nobjects, including higher-order calls. It does not make macros runtime\nfunctions, grant JavaScript `[[Construct]]`, turn a generator into an\nordinary returning function, or add a source feature a frontend refuses.\nConstructors and asynchronous operations keep their declared source\nsemantics; ordinary callability does not imply either capability.\n\n#### 8.10.2 Callable representation and identity\n\nKeep the callable's existing identity and source-specific data. Add the\n---\ndescription: Team roster, role routing, and reviewer-independence rules for datom.world\n---\n\n# DATOM.WORLD SOFTWARE ENGINEERING TEAM\n\nCanonical guide for the shared roster, role routing, and reviewer independence.\nThe Lead Engineering Orchestrator owns coordination, authorization, artifact\nprotocol, and verification in [`orchestrator.md`](./roles/orchestrator.md);\ndelegate session protocol and CLI recipes live in\n[`delegate-invocation-reference.md`](./delegate-invocation-reference.md).\n\n## Roster\n\nThe Orchestrator is deliberately absent from this table; it is the user-facing\nagent that centrally routes tasks to these roles. Each role name links to its\ndelegation prompt template under `docs/agents/roles/`. Review is one role\nbacked by one file, `roles/reviewer.md`, covering adversarial, routine, and\nsecurity-sign-off review via its Adversarial Review and Consensus Follow-up\ntemplates — adjust scope and rigor in the brief per flavor, not the file.\n\n+---------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Role                                              | Responsibility                                                                                         |\n+===================================================+========================================================================================================+\n| [Architect](roles/architect.md)                   | Axioms, invariants, boundaries, architecture                                                           |\n+---------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| [Compiler & AST](roles/compiler-engineer.md)      | AST, lowering, compile-time macros                                                                     |\n+---------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| [Frontend & Graphics](roles/graphics-engineer.md) | Events, WebGL/WebGPU, canvas, terminal                                                                 |\n+---------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| [QA & Verification](roles/qa-engineer.md)         | TDD, parity, lint, regression                                                                          |\n+---------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| [Review](roles/reviewer.md)                       | Adversarial defect discovery, routine correctness/portability checks, and security capability sign-off |\n+---------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| [Scoped / Subagent](roles/subagent.md)            | Bounded searches, edits, docs, lint                                                                    |\n+---------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| [Storage & Indexing](roles/storage-engineer.md)   | B-trees, indexing, DHT, storage, query                                                                 |\n+---------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| [Stream & Network](roles/stream-engineer.md)      | Streams, framing, concurrency, transports, codecs, RPC                                                 |\n+---------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| [VM Runtime](roles/vm-engineer.md)                | CESK, VMs, continuations, macros, loops                                                                |\n+---------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n\n\n## Available Subscriptions & Cost Constraints\n\nThe Orchestrator routes work using these cost profiles. Flat subscriptions are preferred; metered models are reserved for tasks explicitly requiring them.\n\n+-----------------+----------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Provider        | Cost Structure                         | Routing Notes                                                                                               |\n+=================+========================================+=============================================================================================================+\n| agy             | Flat: Included                         | Treated as a flat subscription. Use freely within budget.                                                   |\n+-----------------+----------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| claude          | Inactive / Cancelled                   | Subscription stopped by owner. Do not route tasks here.                                                     |\n+-----------------+----------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| claude-api      | Inactive / Cancelled                   | Anthropic API access stopped by owner. Do not route tasks here.                                             |\n+-----------------+----------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| codex           | Flat: ChatGPT Plus ($20/mo)            | Strict rate limits. Available for any role (architecture, implementation, review, QA).                      |\n|                 |                                        | All codex models (astra, sol, terra, luna) draw ONE shared pool: a cut darkens every seat at once, so       |\n|                 |                                        | sequence codex dispatches rather than fanning them out. Independent parallel capacity is codex | kimi | glm.|\n+-----------------+----------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| deepseek        | Metered: Pay-by-token                  | Flash: $0.14/M in, $0.28/M out. Pro: $0.435/M in, $0.87/M out.                                              |\n|                 |                                        | Off-peak (10:00-01:00, 04:00-06:00 UTC & wknds) is 50% cheaper.                                             |\n|                 |                                        | Cache hits are ~50x cheaper. Reserve for necessary work.                                                    |\n+-----------------+----------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| glm             | Flat: Pro yearly plan ($672/yr)        | Peak hours: weekdays 14:00-18:00 UTC+8. Schedule large jobs off-peak (50% rate).                            |\n+-----------------+----------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| kimi            | Flat: Kimi membership (Plus+)          | Invoke only through the dedicated `kimi` CLI. Monthly quotas apply.                                          |\n|                 |                                        | Pro unlocks Highspeed and 1M context for K3.x.                                                               |\n+-----------------+----------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| mistral         | Flat: Mistral Pro ($20/mo)             | Invoke via `vibe` CLI. Model aliases: mistral-large, mistral-medium-3.5, mistral-4.0, devstral, codestral.   |\n+-----------------+----------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| muse            | Meta login / `META_API_KEY`            | Invoke via `muse` CLI; headless form is `muse exec`. Models:                                                |\n|                 |                                        | `muse-wonder-1.0` (flagship) and `muse-spark-1.3` (fast lane).                                              |\n|                 |                                        | Check [`routing-status.md`](./routing-status.md) before dispatch (geo-restricted at last relay).            |\n+-----------------+----------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n\n## Model Strengths and Selection Guide\n\nThe following guide details the strengths, weaknesses, and optimal use cases for each model based on their current (late 2026) te