{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"agent_message","text":"**Approve hardening-3 as the following engineering plan. Kernel-gate closure remains unapproved.** The baseline is `d5d56ca3`: 100 agreements, two mismatches, two blocked fixtures, two recorded upstream failures, and 107/165 credited arities.\n\nThe plan preserves the executed-arity rule, existing exception departures, upstream pin, and admission denominator. It authorizes no changes to `yang.call` or `yin.vm`.\n\nTwo findings sharpen the brief:\n\n- All three blocked C executions explicitly report **“Guest instruction budget exhausted” at 2,000,000 steps**. This establishes the immediate failure, but does not distinguish legitimate execution cost from repeated work or continuation defects.\n- The deviation classifier still requires a bare closure at `:meta`; refreshed outcomes contain a §8.10 callable descriptor. Restoring declarations therefore requires a representation-aware classifier repair as well as reliable evidence writing. See [the classifier](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:1932).\n\nThe family totals are **11 proof, 4 presyntax, 10 normalization, 17 syntax, 5 typing, and 11 environment/unparser/utils arities**. These are ownership partitions of the initial 58, not promises about additional fixtures needed.\n\n1. **H3-0 — Restore declared dispositions and establish trustworthy evidence. Land before fixture expansion.**\n\n   Scope: the oracle harness, declaration records, evidence writer, accounting regressions, and gate documentation. Preserve declarations as explicit, versioned input to classification; generated evidence must not be their sole source of truth. Recover their provenance from `ea18c8e4^`.\n\n   Carrying a declaration forward must **not** mean copying an old successful disposition onto a changed execution. Revalidate the current fixture against its declared boundary. For the two after-qed rows, check the pinned fixture ID and call, completed A/B/C executions, original exception message, script value and collection shape, and the precise host-function/guest-callable boundary. Validate the guest callable using its actual runtime representation before any lossy evidence rendering; a map containing `{:type :closure}` is insufficient.\n\n   Preserve every original outcome and agreement flag. Do not make C-lane callable representations equal merely to obtain agreement. A declaration whose guard fails remains declared historically, but its current row is a blocker.\n\n   Acceptance:\n   - `elab-after-qed` and `check-after-qed` regain `:recorded-deviation`; the summary contains their complete records.\n   - `typing-ascription` and `compile-malformed` retain their exact guarded upstream-failure dispositions.\n   - All four declared exception departures retain their existing scope and provenance.\n   - Mutated calls, unrelated IDs, changed payloads or shapes, forged callable representations, and incomplete lanes fail classification.\n   - Writer round-trip tests preserve declarations, statuses, raw comparisons, and coverage. Ordinary tests remain read-only.\n   - Refresh the stale gate narrative and verification record: neither 145/165 nor “only 20 remain” describes this baseline.\n\n   With no other changes, the expected result is **100 agreements, two deviations, two blocked fixtures, two upstream failures; 107 credited, 58 remaining**. Any different result requires explanation.\n\n   Routing: QA/harness engineer, with compiler-engineer review of the callable representation. Independent reviewer from a different model family; architect review of the disposition guards.\n\n2. **H3-1 — Diagnose and resolve the two blocked fixtures.**\n\n   Retain both original fixtures, IDs, inputs, and expected A/B results. Smaller diagnostic reproductions supplement them.\n\n   The first diagnostic packet must contain a four-cell comparison—each fixture on each C evaluator—with:\n\n   - Pinned source/compiler/runtime identities, instrumentation mode, configured budget, actual steps, terminal reason, and elapsed time.\n   - Aggregate execution cost and phase boundaries for loading/setup versus fixture execution. Check whether the observer runner resets its counter across consumer invocations; do not assume the current reported count is a whole-fixture total.\n   - Bounded snapshots of control position, continuation depth, call frequencies, and the ordered trace tail at exhaustion.\n   - Instrumented versus uninstrumented results, including the completing semantic run of `check-accepted`.\n   - A minimal reproduction if repeated admission, invocation, or continuation activity appears.\n\n   Collect diagnostics through test-harness facilities. Do not recursively serialize the attached host session or entire closure environments. Partial traces are diagnostic evidence only and contribute **zero** coverage.\n\n   I authorize finite diagnostic trials at **2M, 4M, and 8M instructions**, stopping when sufficient evidence is obtained. These are diagnostic configurations, not an automatic change to the acceptance budget. Before raising that budget, return measured completion costs, instrumentation overhead, progress evidence, and a proposed finite bound with justified headroom. Do not add retries that increase fuel until a fixture passes.\n\n   If execution progresses normally and terminates, a measured harness-budget revision can be reviewed separately. If the fault is in Clojure lowering, repair it with a focused regression. If evidence implicates `yang.call` or `yin.vm`, return the minimized reproduction for an explicit ruling before modifying shared code.\n\n   Acceptance: both original fixtures complete with A/B/C agreement and exact traces under the reviewed finite configuration. Any approved diagnostic-runner changes also demonstrate that exhaustion remains a blocker.\n\n   Routing: compiler engineer leads; VM engineer provides diagnosis where needed, without shared-runtime write authority. Architect reviews the root-cause packet and any acceptance-budget change.\n\n3. **H3-2 — Close proof elaboration through a small named integration family.**\n\n   **Yes: use end-to-end proof fixtures to cover the elab cluster together.** Do not start by manufacturing eleven isolated helper calls.\n\n   Begin with the repaired `check-assume-have-discharge` fixture. Its structure already follows the pinned upstream proof tests: declarations, two local definitions with a reference between them, discharge, and final checking. Measure what it actually credits before adding fixtures.\n\n   Build the smallest complementary family covering:\n   - Named `have` with explicit and inferred types, plus anonymous `have`.\n   - A local definition referenced by another local definition.\n   - Discharge with nonempty dependency sets, forcing definition abstraction and rewriting of local calls.\n   - Completion through `qed` and `check-proof`.\n\n   This family targets all eleven listed proof arities. Register integration IDs explicitly. Body-entry evidence must show the exact arity in **both** C lanes; static dependencies and successful B/C-only freshness tests confer no differential credit.\n\n   Add source-backed refusal companions for meaningful boundaries, such as duplicate local definitions, invalid declared types, and incorrect discharge. Preserve the fresh-supply and immutable-prior-context regressions. In particular, preserve pinned upstream behavior such as its conservative `update-var-deps`; this slice does not “correct” LaTTe.\n\n   Acceptance: all eleven proof targets have passing direct or named-integration witnesses, with concrete expected proof/type outcomes and relevant state assertions. If a helper remains unentered, add a minimal corpus-derived helper fixture for that residual. If meaningful state cannot be compared under the existing context adaptation, return the proposed comparison boundary for review rather than discarding fields.\n\n   Routing: compiler/proof engineer; QA independently reconstructs the credit set. Review must examine outcome semantics as well as traces.\n\n4. **H3-3a — Close residual syntax utilities; H3-3b — close environment, unparser, and utilities.**\n\n   These are separately reviewable fixture slices. Their initial target sets are 17 and 11 arities respectively; subtract credits already established by H3-1/H3-2.\n\n   | Family | Fixture strategy | Required observations |\n   |---|---|---|\n   | Syntax, 17 | Prefer direct entry fixtures for remaining wrappers and overloads. Use named capture/substitution or alpha-normalization integrations for internal helpers when they actually execute. | Alpha-equivalent and distinct terms; capture avoidance; bound/free variables; freshness collisions; binder/readable-term structure; empty/nonempty assumptions. Enter each remaining overload explicitly. |\n   | Environment, 6 | Direct predicates and `registered-definition?` overloads; explicit-context fixtures for `qualify-def`. | True/false predicates, found/missing definitions, local/global distinction, qualification. No ambient namespace lookup added. |\n   | Unparser, 1 | Named register → use → unregister → use sequence. | Removal changes subsequent unparsing; the previous immutable B/C context remains usable. Preserve the documented extra-context arity mapping. |\n   | Utilities, 4 | Direct `map-with`, `vconcat`, `vcons`, and `zip`. | Concrete nonempty outputs, empty/boundary behavior where relevant, and collection shapes. Exercise an admitted guest callable through `map-with`. |\n\n   Prefer fixtures from the pinned upstream tests. Hand-built inputs are appropriate for missing overloads and isolation boundaries, provided they are checked against A and have a concrete expected result. Do not manufacture exceptions for total constructors or utilities.\n\n   Acceptance: every target still assigned to each sub-slice has a valid witness, existing credits remain supported, and no equality projection is broadened.\n\n   Routing: QA/fixture engineer. Escalate actual lowering or adaptation defects to the compiler engineer rather than hiding them in expected outputs.\n\n5. **H3-4 — Close residual presyntax and typing.**\n\n   Initial targets: four presyntax and five typing arities.\n\n   `parse/1` gets a **direct fixture**. The existing poisoned-`parse/1` regression remains: successful execution of `parse/2` must never establish `parse/1`.\n\n   Prefer named integrations for the remaining parser helpers: parse a product, an arrow, and an explicitly registered defined term, then inspect the parsed term and type-check it. For typing, use a parameterized definition/application that exercises argument preparation, generalization, reference typing, and application typing. Add direct residual fixtures only where those integrations do not enter the required helper.\n\n   Corpus fixtures supply the starting terms and expected structures. Cover relevant invalid applications or argument failures without using the declared `typing-ascription` failure as credit.\n\n   Acceptance: all nine initial targets, less earlier credits, are supported by exact passing traces; parser output and inferred types match the pinned oracle. Unknown names, local/global lookup, and argument substitutions retain their existing semantics.\n\n   Routing: compiler engineer with QA fixture support and independent semantic review.\n\n6. **H3-5 — Close residual normalization.**\n\n   Initial targets: ten arities across `norm` and `nbe`.\n\n   Use direct fixtures for `beta-reduction/1`, `beta-step/1`, `beta-step/2`, `redex?/1`, and `nbe/evaluation/1` when still uncredited. Use compact named integrations for argument reduction and explicit parameterized-definition instantiation. Treat `norm/instantiate-def/3` and `nbe/instantiate-def/3` as separate targets.\n\n   Fixtures must include reducible and irreducible terms, a nonempty argument path, and explicit local/global definitions where applicable. Preserve opacity behavior and the documented upstream `delta-step` local-resolution limitation. Do not introduce ambient-only resolution or repair upstream behavior to simplify agreement.\n\n   Acceptance: all remaining normalization targets are credited, expected normal forms and refusal outcomes match A/B/C, and every fixture terminates within its reviewed finite budget.\n\n   Routing: compiler/normalization engineer; QA independently audits traces and expected values.\n\n7. **H3-6 — Final evidence audit and closure review.**\n\n   Regenerate from the final reviewed tree, then run the full oracle in a fresh process. Record the actual source commit, relevant source/configuration digests, upstream pin, host/runtime, budgets, fixture inventory, outcomes, and raw traces. The existing `:base \"7dc18478\" :working-tree true` field alone is inadequate final provenance.\n\n   An independent audit must reconstruct credits from raw traces rather than trusting `:coverage :entries`. It must establish:\n\n   - **165/165 admitted arities credited; zero unexercised.**\n   - Every credited path names a passing fixture and demonstrates the exact mapped arity in both C lanes.\n   - Zero unexplained mismatches or blocked fixtures.\n   - Both after-qed deviations and both upstream-failure dispositions remain declared and guarded; none contributes coverage.\n   - All original fixtures and the twenty established residual-consumer contracts remain exercised.\n   - Admission remains 155 bindings partitioned 146/9, and 175 candidate arities partitioned 165/10, unless separately amended.\n\n   Keep the final `(= :pass (:gate-status evidence))` assertion unchanged. Passing it is necessary for closure; independent review and final architect sign-off complete acceptance.\n\n**Ordering and intermediate states.** Land H3-0 first. H3-1 informs the proof integration work; H3-2 precedes residual fixture expansion so later slices work from measured gaps. Independent fixture preparation may overlap, but shared harness changes and evidence refreshes need one integration owner.\n\nAfter every slice, refresh evidence and publish newly credited, withdrawn, and remaining arity sets with fixture IDs. Do not promise monotonic credit: any withdrawn witness must be explained. Restoring declarations does not raise the agreement count; resolving the original blockers can restore the original corpus to 102 agreements, but the resulting coverage must be measured.\n\nThe gate remains `:blocked` while **any** admitted arity lacks a witness, any unexplained execution failure remains, or a required disposition fails validation. Intermediate slice sign-off is explicitly scoped and does not constitute kernel closure. Do not suppress the nonzero oracle result or describe it as green.\n\n**Verification for every engineering slice.** Run focused tests while iterating, then the full oracle at the slice checkpoint. Preserve the missing-lane, unused-arity, poisoned-`parse/1`, disposition-mutation, and existing consumer regressions. After H3-0, any expected remaining failure must be identified by assertion; new failures require diagnosis.\n\nCompiler changes additionally require the Clojure compiler, macro, stream-evaluation, and store-write-audit regression suites, including common-call admission, refusal termination, outcome propagation, and tail-depth controls. Run scoped kondo, formatting, and whitespace checks. Follow the repository’s affected-test tri-host gates at the final slice tip; JVM sharded feedback alone is insufficient. The LaTTe differential claim itself remains **both C evaluators on JVM** unless a separate host-oracle expansion is approved. Record Node/Dart verification with its actual scope. See [build and test rules](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/agents/build-n-test.md).\n\n**Admission stays closed to incidental change.** The nine excluded bindings comprise eight output/formatting bindings and `nano-time`; `unparser/show-term` has two arities, producing ten excluded candidates.\n\nReopening requires either a demonstrated inventory/classification error or an explicitly designed portable capability that removes the stated exclusion reason. Output admission would need an explicit stream and defined formatting observations; clock admission would need an explicit clock input/capability and a defensible differential comparison. Host stdout capture or access to a host clock alone does not establish portability.\n\nAny amendment requires separate architect review, exact binding/arity changes, capability and comparison contracts, fixtures, and regenerated inventories. Newly admitted arities enter the denominator immediately. Difficulty, budget exhaustion, apparent non-use, or proximity to 100% never justifies excluding an admitted arity. Changes to frozen Gate S v1 rules or exclusions require their own specification version; this compiler differential closure grants no such change.\n\nThis was a read-only design pass. I inspected the current evidence, harness, historical dispositions, and pinned upstream proof tests; I did not rerun the oracle or modify the checkout."}}
